<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<phpunit xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         bootstrap="web/core/tests/bootstrap.php"
         colors="true"
         beStrictAboutTestsThatDoNotTestAnything="true"
         beStrictAboutOutputDuringTests="true"
         beStrictAboutChangesToGlobalState="true"
         failOnRisky="true"
         failOnWarning="true"
         cacheResult="false"
         failOnDeprecation="true"
         xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://schema.phpunit.de/11.5/phpunit.xsd">
  <php>
    <ini name="error_reporting" value="32767"/>
    <ini name="memory_limit" value="-1"/>
    <env name="SIMPLETEST_DB" value="sqlite://localhost/sites/simpletest/test.sqlite"/>
    <env name="SIMPLETEST_BASE_URL" value="http://localhost"/>
    <env name="BROWSERTEST_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY" value="/tmp"/>
    <!--
      SYMFONY_DEPRECATIONS_HELPER is gone, and with it twenty lines of
      comment weighing `weak` against `disabled` against `max[total]=0`.
      All of it was inert: symfony/phpunit-bridge is not a dependency of
      this package and is not in vendor/, so nothing ever read the
      variable. The deprecations this suite reports are PHPUnit 11's own.

      That is why #137 - REQUIREMENT_OK removed in drupal:12.0.0 - was
      found by a code review rather than by CI. The guard was never there;
      it only looked like it was.

      Deprecations now fail the build, but only the ones this module
      causes: the failOn flag on the root element, and
      ignoreIndirectDeprecations on <source>, whose include list is src/.

      Measured 2026-09-22 on a full run: 22 PHP deprecations, 20 of them
      PHP 8.4 implicit-nullable notices inside
      mundschenk-at/php-typography, 2 null-array-offset notices inside
      core. NONE from src/. So this costs nothing today, and failing on
      third-party deprecations instead would turn CI red on a day nobody
      touched this repo.

      Know what this does NOT catch: a deprecation this module raises
      itself with @trigger_error(), which is how Drupal raises them.
      PHPUnit ignores suppressed deprecations by default, and the switch
      that changes it - ignoreSuppressionOfDeprecations on <source> -
      would un-suppress core's as well. So the guard covers PHP
      deprecations caused by our code (an implicitly nullable parameter,
      a null array offset), not our own deprecation notices. Verified by
      mutation: an unsuppressed trigger_error() in src/ exits 1, the same
      call with @ exits 0.
    -->
  </php>
  <testsuites>
    <testsuite name="unit">
      <directory>tests/src/Unit</directory>
    </testsuite>
    <testsuite name="kernel">
      <directory>tests/src/Kernel</directory>
    </testsuite>
  </testsuites>
  <source ignoreIndirectDeprecations="true">
    <include>
      <directory>src</directory>
    </include>
  </source>
</phpunit>
